data: {"type": "assistant_msg", "ts": "2026-08-06T12:25:54.778539+00:00", "content": "Based on the available live telemetry, **no evidence of a BGP session flap or degraded BGP state on ce_branch24 or its immediate upstream peer (pe12) is visible in the current investigation window**.\n\n**Evidence gathered:**\n\n1. **ce_branch24 itself exposes no BGP metrics** \u2014 the device only reports SD-WAN tunnel telemetry (latency, jitter, loss, rekeys) to six hub sites and interface counters [metrics:0\u201323 from tunnel query]. Hardware health (fan, power, temp) and queue stats are normal [metrics:0\u20137 from general query].\n\n2. **Upstream PE router pe12 (1 hop away) shows healthy BGP** \u2014 two peers established (`bgp_peer_established=2`), actively exchanging messages (`bgp_msg_rx_total=2062`, `bgp_msg_tx_total=1724`), and advertising 67 prefixes in each of the CORP and VOICE VRFs [metrics:0\u20133 from pe12 bgp query; topo:pe12].\n\n3. **Topology walk confirms pe12 is the direct BGP-speaking neighbor**; no other BGP-speaking nodes appear within 2 hops [topo:ce_branch24, topo:pe12].\n\n4. **Logs and historical incident search returned no records** for \u201cBGP\u201d, \u201cbgp\u201d, or \u201cflap\u201d on either ce_branch24 or pe12 in the current window [search_logs on both devices].\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe live data shows BGP sessions on the upstream PE (pe12) are **established and actively passing traffic** with no flap signature (no peer-down counters, no prefix withdrawal spikes). Since ce_branch24 is a branch SD-WAN edge that terminates tunnels to hubs rather than speaking BGP directly, the \u201cBGP session flap\u201d referenced in the question either occurred outside the current telemetry window or was resolved cleanly \u2014 the present state is **not degraded**."}

